      0,
      'Gale and Shapley\'s 1962 algorithm has an asymmetric incentive property, later sharpened by Dubins-Freedman and by Alvin Roth\'s applied work: the proposing side has no incentive to misrepresent its preferences, since the algorithm hands proposers their most-preferred outcome among all stable matchings, while the receiving side can sometimes benefit from strategic misreporting. Stable matchings are also not generally unique, though the algorithm always selects the proposer-optimal one from among whichever stable matchings exist. This machinery underlies real institutions such as the US National Resident Matching Program for doctors and several big-city school-choice systems (Boston and New York among them). Lloyd Shapley shared the 2012 Nobel Prize with Alvin Roth for turning this theory into working market designs; David Gale had died in 2008, and the Nobel is never awarded posthumously.',
