      0,
      'Coase\'s 1972 informal observation, later formalized by Stokey (1981), Bulow (1982) and Gul-Sonnenschein-Wilson (1986), is that a durable-goods seller who cannot commit to future prices effectively competes against its own future self: high-valuation buyers, anticipating that unsold inventory will force price cuts later, simply wait, and as revision opportunities become more frequent this erosion accelerates until price and profit collapse toward the competitive benchmark. Real firms fight this by adopting commitment devices the model doesn\'t allow for by assumption - leasing instead of selling, planned obsolescence, or limited-edition releases - which is exactly why some famous durable-goods sellers historically leased rather than sold their product outright. IBM\'s decades-long practice of leasing rather than selling its mainframe computers is the textbook illustration of a firm sidestepping this exact commitment problem.',
