      'Hotelling\'s own 1929 paper is widely credited with predicting that competitors cluster near the center of a market - the intuition often invoked for why gas stations or political parties converge - but this is a well-known case where the celebrated result doesn\'t actually survive rigorous modeling: d\'Aspremont, Gabszewicz and Thisse showed in 1979 that with the correctly specified two-stage game and quadratic transport costs, the unique subgame-perfect equilibrium instead has firms locating at the two extreme ends of the market, maximizing rather than minimizing differentiation, in order to soften the price competition that follows. The instability traces to the fact that with linear transport costs the price subgame is discontinuous, generating Bertrand-style undercutting incentives that break down the equilibrium Hotelling originally claimed to find. It\'s a favorite teaching example of a widely cited \'textbook result\' that the original source doesn\'t actually deliver once checked with modern equilibrium tools.',
